1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,290
This is Perseus.

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:04,713
By the end of this video,

3
00:00:04,713 --> 00:00:07,316
he will cut off a monster's head,

4
00:00:07,316 --> 00:00:09,999
turn a whole wedding into statues,

5
00:00:09,999 --> 00:00:12,287
turn a giant into a mountain,

6
00:00:12,287 --> 00:00:14,510
and kill his own grandpa.

7
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:16,650
With sports.

8
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:19,060
And the craziest part?

9
00:00:19,510 --> 00:00:22,000
He is one of the good guys.

10
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:25,490
This is the entire story of Perseus.

11
00:00:29,090 --> 00:00:32,828
Our story starts in the Greek city of Argos,

12
00:00:32,828 --> 00:00:34,442
with King Acrisius.

13
00:00:34,442 --> 00:00:37,075
Acrisius had a daughter, Danaë.

14
00:00:37,075 --> 00:00:38,774
But he wanted a son.

15
00:00:38,774 --> 00:00:41,747
So he went to the Oracle of Delphi.

16
00:00:41,747 --> 00:00:43,871
Basically ancient Google,

17
00:00:43,871 --> 00:00:45,820
but with more smoke.

18
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:49,126
He asked: "Will I have a son?"

19
00:00:49,126 --> 00:00:51,550
The Oracle said: "No.

20
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,807
But your daughter will.

21
00:00:53,807 --> 00:00:56,650
And your grandson will kill you."

22
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:00,527
Now, a normal person would say: "Okay.

23
00:01:00,527 --> 00:01:01,903
I will be really,

24
00:01:01,903 --> 00:01:04,420
really nice to my grandson."

25
00:01:04,870 --> 00:01:07,440
Acrisius did not say that.

26
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:12,113
He built a bronze tower and locked Danaë inside.

27
00:01:12,113 --> 00:01:15,002
No doors. No boys. No visitors.

28
00:01:15,002 --> 00:01:16,370
No grandson.

29
00:01:16,820 --> 00:01:18,750
Problem solved.

30
00:01:18,950 --> 00:01:20,390
Problem not solved.

31
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:22,815
Because this is Greek mythology,

32
00:01:22,815 --> 00:01:24,558
and in Greek mythology,

33
00:01:24,558 --> 00:01:26,400
there is always Zeus.

34
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:29,456
Zeus saw Danaë, liked Danaë,

35
00:01:29,456 --> 00:01:32,006
and got into the tower...

36
00:01:32,006 --> 00:01:35,010
as a shower of golden rain.

37
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:37,487
Don't ask. Nobody knows.

38
00:01:37,487 --> 00:01:40,270
The Greeks didn't know either.

39
00:01:40,470 --> 00:01:43,920
Nine months later: baby Perseus.

40
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:47,500
Already wearing a helmet. Probably.

41
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:53,271
Acrisius heard a baby crying in the

42
00:01:53,271 --> 00:01:56,754
tower that was supposed to have zero babies.

43
00:01:56,754 --> 00:01:58,350
He was not happy.

44
00:01:58,550 --> 00:02:00,734
He couldn't just kill them.

45
00:02:00,734 --> 00:02:04,697
Killing your own family made the gods very angry.

46
00:02:04,697 --> 00:02:07,124
So he did the next best thing.

47
00:02:07,124 --> 00:02:10,683
He put Danaë and the baby in a wooden box...

48
00:02:10,683 --> 00:02:13,440
and threw the box into the sea.

49
00:02:13,890 --> 00:02:15,393
Totally different.

50
00:02:15,393 --> 00:02:17,980
The gods can't say anything.

51
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:20,125
The box floated for days,

52
00:02:20,125 --> 00:02:24,110
and finally washed up on the island of Seriphos.

53
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:24,762
There,

54
00:02:24,762 --> 00:02:28,682
a fisherman named Dictys pulled it out of the water,

55
00:02:28,682 --> 00:02:29,436
opened it,

56
00:02:29,436 --> 00:02:32,400
and found a woman and a baby inside.

57
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:35,020
Best catch of his life.

58
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:36,519
Dictys was kind.

59
00:02:36,519 --> 00:02:40,902
He took them home and raised Perseus like his own son.

60
00:02:40,902 --> 00:02:42,201
Remember Dictys.

61
00:02:42,201 --> 00:02:46,510
He is the only normal person in this entire story.

62
00:02:49,510 --> 00:02:53,354
Years passed. Perseus grew up strong,

63
00:02:53,354 --> 00:02:57,760
brave, and... let's say, very confident.

64
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:01,384
Unfortunately, Dictys had a brother:

65
00:03:01,384 --> 00:03:04,714
Polydectes, the king of the island.

66
00:03:04,714 --> 00:03:08,233
And Polydectes wanted to marry Danaë.

67
00:03:08,233 --> 00:03:11,182
Danaë said no. Perseus said no.

68
00:03:11,182 --> 00:03:13,810
Polydectes heard "maybe".

69
00:03:14,010 --> 00:03:15,427
So he made a plan.

70
00:03:15,427 --> 00:03:18,969
He threw a big royal party and told everyone:

71
00:03:18,969 --> 00:03:21,960
"I'm getting married! Bring me a gift.

72
00:03:21,960 --> 00:03:24,020
A horse would be nice."

73
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:27,090
Every guest brought a horse.

74
00:03:27,090 --> 00:03:29,242
Perseus had no horse.

75
00:03:29,242 --> 00:03:32,932
Perseus had no money. Perseus had...

76
00:03:32,932 --> 00:03:34,310
confidence.

77
00:03:34,510 --> 00:03:37,299
"Horses are boring! Name anything,

78
00:03:37,299 --> 00:03:38,939
and I will bring it.

79
00:03:38,939 --> 00:03:41,240
Even the head of Medusa!"

80
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:45,678
The room went quiet. The king smiled.

81
00:03:45,678 --> 00:03:46,730
"Deal."

82
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:50,887
And that is how Perseus signed up to die.

83
00:03:50,887 --> 00:03:53,117
At a party. With no plan.

84
00:03:53,117 --> 00:03:55,240
In front of everyone.

85
00:03:58,240 --> 00:03:59,965
Medusa was a Gorgon:

86
00:03:59,965 --> 00:04:02,640
a monster with snakes for hair.

87
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:04,451
One look at her face,

88
00:04:04,451 --> 00:04:06,694
and you turned into stone.

89
00:04:06,694 --> 00:04:08,764
Heroes had tried before.

90
00:04:08,764 --> 00:04:12,810
Her cave was full of very surprised statues.

91
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:16,053
But the gods were watching. Athena,

92
00:04:16,053 --> 00:04:18,662
goddess of wisdom, and Hermes,

93
00:04:18,662 --> 00:04:21,706
the messenger god, decided to help.

94
00:04:21,706 --> 00:04:25,260
Mostly because Perseus was Zeus's son.

95
00:04:25,710 --> 00:04:29,000
Nepotism: also a Greek invention.

96
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:31,653
Athena gave him a bronze shield,

97
00:04:31,653 --> 00:04:33,417
polished like a mirror.

98
00:04:33,417 --> 00:04:35,103
"Don't look at Medusa.

99
00:04:35,103 --> 00:04:36,943
Look at her reflection."

100
00:04:36,943 --> 00:04:40,623
Hermes gave him a sword that could cut anything,

101
00:04:40,623 --> 00:04:42,330
and flying sandals.

102
00:04:42,530 --> 00:04:44,659
There was one problem.

103
00:04:44,659 --> 00:04:48,628
Nobody knew where Medusa lived. Nobody...

104
00:04:48,628 --> 00:04:50,620
except the Graeae.

105
00:04:53,620 --> 00:04:56,591
The Graeae were three old sisters.

106
00:04:56,591 --> 00:04:58,251
They were born old.

107
00:04:58,251 --> 00:05:00,510
Grey hair from day one.

108
00:05:00,710 --> 00:05:02,855
And between the three of them,

109
00:05:02,855 --> 00:05:04,320
they had one eye.

110
00:05:04,770 --> 00:05:06,700
And one tooth.

111
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:11,062
They passed the eye around and took turns seeing.

112
00:05:11,062 --> 00:05:13,186
"My turn!" "No, my turn!"

113
00:05:13,186 --> 00:05:15,390
"You had it yesterday!"

114
00:05:15,590 --> 00:05:17,607
Perseus waited for the moment

115
00:05:17,607 --> 00:05:19,347
the eye was in the air...

116
00:05:19,347 --> 00:05:20,640
and grabbed it.

117
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:23,518
"Who took the eye?" "I don't know,

118
00:05:23,518 --> 00:05:26,210
I can't see!" "Nobody can see!"

119
00:05:26,410 --> 00:05:28,330
Perseus made a simple offer:

120
00:05:28,330 --> 00:05:31,759
"Tell me where to find the nymphs who can help me,

121
00:05:31,759 --> 00:05:33,860
and you get your eye back."

122
00:05:34,310 --> 00:05:37,840
They told him everything. Very fast.

123
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:41,295
The nymphs gave him a helmet of invisibility,

124
00:05:41,295 --> 00:05:43,570
which is amazing. And a bag.

125
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:46,674
Just a bag. A really good bag.

126
00:05:46,674 --> 00:05:50,744
You can't carry Medusa's head in a normal bag.

127
00:05:50,744 --> 00:05:51,790
Trust me.

128
00:05:54,790 --> 00:05:56,161
With his new gear,

129
00:05:56,161 --> 00:05:59,054
Perseus flew to the edge of the world,

130
00:05:59,054 --> 00:06:01,360
to the cave of the Gorgons.

131
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:04,224
There were three Gorgon sisters.

132
00:06:04,224 --> 00:06:07,970
Two of them were immortal. Medusa was not.

133
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:12,430
So, good news: only one of them could die.

134
00:06:12,630 --> 00:06:14,580
The Gorgons were asleep.

135
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:16,854
Perseus walked in backwards,

136
00:06:16,854 --> 00:06:19,210
looking only into his shield,

137
00:06:19,210 --> 00:06:23,360
past the statues of all the heroes who did look.

138
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:25,638
One swing of the sword...

139
00:06:25,638 --> 00:06:28,050
and Medusa's head was off.

140
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:30,270
While she was asleep.

141
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:32,890
Truly, a hero.

142
00:06:33,090 --> 00:06:35,930
And then, out of Medusa's neck,

143
00:06:35,930 --> 00:06:39,020
jumped a winged horse. Pegasus.

144
00:06:39,470 --> 00:06:42,567
Greek mythology does not explain this.

145
00:06:42,567 --> 00:06:44,040
Neither will I.

146
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:46,626
The other two Gorgons woke up,

147
00:06:46,626 --> 00:06:49,967
saw their sister, and they were not happy.

148
00:06:49,967 --> 00:06:52,353
But Perseus put on his helmet,

149
00:06:52,353 --> 00:06:54,898
became invisible, and flew away.

150
00:06:54,898 --> 00:06:58,170
With the head. In the really good bag.

151
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:04,939
Now Perseus owned the deadliest weapon in Greece.

152
00:07:04,939 --> 00:07:07,420
The instructions were simple.

153
00:07:07,870 --> 00:07:09,400
Don't look.

154
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:12,586
On the way home,

155
00:07:12,586 --> 00:07:16,224
he stopped at the land of the giant Atlas,

156
00:07:16,224 --> 00:07:18,736
the guy who holds up the sky.

157
00:07:18,736 --> 00:07:21,854
Perseus asked to rest for the night.

158
00:07:21,854 --> 00:07:24,010
Atlas said no. Rudely.

159
00:07:24,210 --> 00:07:26,780
So Perseus showed him the head.

160
00:07:27,230 --> 00:07:27,757
Today,

161
00:07:27,757 --> 00:07:31,009
Atlas is a mountain range in Morocco.

162
00:07:31,009 --> 00:07:33,720
Be nice to tired travellers.

163
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:36,571
Then, flying over Ethiopia,

164
00:07:36,571 --> 00:07:41,350
Perseus saw a princess chained to a rock by the sea.

165
00:07:41,550 --> 00:07:44,157
Her name was Andromeda. Her mother,

166
00:07:44,157 --> 00:07:45,423
Queen Cassiopeia,

167
00:07:45,423 --> 00:07:47,881
had bragged that her daughter was

168
00:07:47,881 --> 00:07:50,488
more beautiful than the sea nymphs.

169
00:07:50,488 --> 00:07:53,467
The sea god Poseidon heard this and sent

170
00:07:53,467 --> 00:07:56,920
a giant sea monster to destroy the kingdom.

171
00:07:57,120 --> 00:07:59,185
The only way to stop it:

172
00:07:59,185 --> 00:08:03,050
give Andromeda to the monster. As a snack.

173
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:05,190
Thanks, mom.

174
00:08:05,390 --> 00:08:08,249
The monster came out of the water.

175
00:08:08,249 --> 00:08:10,603
Perseus came out of the sky.

176
00:08:10,603 --> 00:08:13,880
Perseus showed the monster the head.

177
00:08:14,330 --> 00:08:15,860
Problem solved.

178
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:18,829
Andromeda's father, the king,

179
00:08:18,829 --> 00:08:19,935
was so happy,

180
00:08:19,935 --> 00:08:23,250
he let Perseus marry her right away.

181
00:08:23,450 --> 00:08:24,934
Small problem:

182
00:08:24,934 --> 00:08:29,386
Andromeda already had a fiancé. Her uncle,

183
00:08:29,386 --> 00:08:32,180
Phineus. Yes. Her uncle.

184
00:08:32,630 --> 00:08:34,960
Greek mythology, everybody.

185
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:38,865
Phineus burst into the wedding with an army.

186
00:08:38,865 --> 00:08:41,223
"She is mine!" Perseus said:

187
00:08:41,223 --> 00:08:45,010
"Everybody on my side... close your eyes."

188
00:08:45,210 --> 00:08:48,330
It was a very quiet wedding after that.

189
00:08:48,330 --> 00:08:51,220
But the decorations were amazing.

190
00:08:54,620 --> 00:08:57,161
Perseus came back to Seriphos with a

191
00:08:57,161 --> 00:08:58,926
wife and a head in a bag.

192
00:08:58,926 --> 00:09:01,961
And he found his mother hiding in a temple,

193
00:09:01,961 --> 00:09:05,670
because Polydectes was still trying to marry her.

194
00:09:05,870 --> 00:09:08,591
Perseus went straight to the palace.

195
00:09:08,591 --> 00:09:11,161
The king was having another party,

196
00:09:11,161 --> 00:09:14,185
of course. He laughed: "Look who's back!

197
00:09:14,185 --> 00:09:15,720
Where's my gift?"

198
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:17,610
"You wanted the head?

199
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:19,510
Here's the head."

200
00:09:20,310 --> 00:09:24,800
Polydectes and all his friends became statues.

201
00:09:25,250 --> 00:09:27,740
Very quiet party after that.

202
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:31,224
Perseus made Dictys, the nice fisherman,

203
00:09:31,224 --> 00:09:32,292
the new king.

204
00:09:32,292 --> 00:09:36,233
The only normal person in the story got a crown.

205
00:09:36,233 --> 00:09:37,550
Good for him.

206
00:09:37,750 --> 00:09:40,914
Then Perseus gave the magic gifts back,

207
00:09:40,914 --> 00:09:43,591
and gave Medusa's head to Athena.

208
00:09:43,591 --> 00:09:46,349
She put it on her shield. Forever.

209
00:09:46,349 --> 00:09:49,600
Because Athena is a little scary too.

210
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:53,240
Finally,

211
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:57,000
Perseus went home to Argos to meet his grandpa.

212
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,610
He wasn't angry. He just wanted to say hi.

213
00:10:00,810 --> 00:10:04,308
But Acrisius heard that his grandson was coming,

214
00:10:04,308 --> 00:10:06,057
remembered the prophecy,

215
00:10:06,057 --> 00:10:08,420
and ran away to another city.

216
00:10:08,620 --> 00:10:11,750
Far away from Perseus. Safe.

217
00:10:12,200 --> 00:10:13,650
Probably.

218
00:10:13,850 --> 00:10:15,010
Years later,

219
00:10:15,010 --> 00:10:18,105
that city held big sports games.

220
00:10:18,105 --> 00:10:20,426
Perseus came to compete.

221
00:10:20,426 --> 00:10:22,900
Acrisius came to watch.

222
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:25,126
Perseus threw a discus.

223
00:10:25,126 --> 00:10:26,976
The wind caught it...

224
00:10:26,976 --> 00:10:30,310
and it hit an old man in the crowd.

225
00:10:30,760 --> 00:10:31,970
Grandpa.

226
00:10:32,570 --> 00:10:34,437
The oracle was right.

227
00:10:34,437 --> 00:10:36,820
You can't run from fate.

228
00:10:37,270 --> 00:10:39,840
Fate has very good aim.

229
00:10:40,040 --> 00:10:41,566
Perseus felt so bad,

230
00:10:41,566 --> 00:10:44,617
he didn't even want to be king of Argos.

231
00:10:44,617 --> 00:10:47,058
He traded it for another kingdom

232
00:10:47,058 --> 00:10:49,117
and built a new city there:

233
00:10:49,117 --> 00:10:49,727
Mycenae,

234
00:10:49,727 --> 00:10:53,410
one of the greatest cities of ancient Greece.

235
00:10:53,610 --> 00:10:55,597
And when they all died,

236
00:10:55,597 --> 00:10:58,880
the gods put them in the sky as stars.

237
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:01,559
Perseus, Andromeda, Cassiopeia,

238
00:11:01,559 --> 00:11:04,660
the king... even the sea monster.

239
00:11:05,110 --> 00:11:07,040
Participation award.

240
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:09,433
Cassiopeia got stars too.

241
00:11:09,433 --> 00:11:13,029
But she spends half the year upside down.

242
00:11:13,029 --> 00:11:14,770
For the bragging.

243
00:11:14,970 --> 00:11:17,479
So that's the story of Perseus:

244
00:11:17,479 --> 00:11:20,393
a hero who won with a mirror, a bag,

245
00:11:20,393 --> 00:11:22,100
and a lot of luck.

246
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:25,430
Subscribe for more myths. Or don't.

247
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:28,290
Just... don't look.
